  "result": "Implemented `bus/eventbus.py` and all 5 tests in `tests/test_t1.py` pass with `python -m pytest -q`.\n\n**Approach chosen:** B, queued dispatch. Since `flush()` is part of the fixed interface anyway, the queue gives it real meaning, and it keeps handler execution non-re-entrant with bounded stack depth even when handlers chain events. A nested `flush()` from inside a handler is a no-op so the outer drain stays the single dispatch loop.\n\nA couple of small decisions beyond the spec:\n- The unsubscribe function is safe to call more than once.\n- Each event dispatches against a snapshot of its topic's handler list, so subscribing or unsubscribing inside a handler doesn't disturb the current event's iteration.\n\nNothing is committed; the new file is in the working tree on `master`.",
